# tools/interactive/lib.exp -- reusable expect helpers for the LS-CI
# interactive regression net (docs/LIFE-SUPPORT.md, "LS-CI"). Sourced by the
# tools/interactive/*.exp scenarios; the scenarios stay short + declarative.
#
# This drives a REAL PTY into QEMU's `-serial mon:stdio` console -- the ONLY
# way to inject console keystrokes. A piped stdin hits EOF and closes the
# chardev, which is exactly why CI never exercised the interactive path and
# why LS-1 (UART RX master-disabled) and LS-2 (external output dropped) both
# shipped silently. This harness is the standing guard against that class.
#
# Echo discipline (the central gotcha, ground-truthed against the real console):
# the ut line editor REDRAWS THE PROMPT on every keystroke (`ESC[K` + the colored
# `/ ⊢ ` + a cursor-forward), and renders the typed text via terminal screen
# state -- it does NOT emit the typed line as plain contiguous bytes. So the
# typed command CANNOT be matched from the byte stream; only the command's
# OUTPUT, which prints as clean plain text on its own line after Enter, is
# matchable. Every matcher here therefore asserts on OUTPUT only. To stay robust
# even if the editor ever changes to echo input verbatim, prefer an output token
# that cannot appear in the typed line anyway -- a `tr a-z A-Z` upper-cased token,
# or a `cat: ` stderr prefix.
#
# Persistent-state discipline (#85 -- read this before writing a scenario that
# writes to the pool). Every scenario boots against build/fixtures/pool.img and
# MUTATES it. Since #85 the harness restores a pristine clone before every
# ATTEMPT, so contamination no longer crosses scenario boundaries -- but two
# rules still bind, because the restore is per-attempt, not per-boot:
#
#   1. HEAL AT START, never only at END. `lc_fail` is an `exit` -- no
#      end-of-scenario cleanup EVER runs on the failing path, which is precisely
#      how a failed ls-gfx-mode left `mode 1600 900` behind and broke geometry
#      asserts in every later boot (#82). If your scenario writes persistent
#      state, delete/normalize it in the FIRST boot before asserting anything.
#   2. Deleting the target is what makes an assertion mean something. A leg that
#      asserts "file X exists" without removing X first cannot distinguish "the
#      mechanism works" from "something wrote this once, long ago" -- ls-gfx-play
#      passed for five days that way, after the path it asserted had stopped
#      being written (#83). A stale artifact must not be able to satisfy you.
#
# A scenario with its OWN multiple boots (ls-gfx-mode/-font/-osd-persist persist
# state in boot 1 and read it back in boot 2) is unaffected: the restore fires
# between attempts, never between a scenario's own boots.
#
# Env knobs (set by tools/test-interactive.sh):
#   LS_CI_BOOT_TIMEOUT  seconds to reach the shell after spawn (default 180)
#   LS_CI_CMD_TIMEOUT   seconds for a single command's output (default 30)
#   LS_CI_STEPS         flush-immune step-marker file (optional)
#   THYLACINE_ACCEL     hvf|tcg (forwarded to run-vm.sh; default tcg here)
#
# The full session transcript is captured by the wrapper redirecting expect's
# stdout to a file (log_user stays on). We deliberately do NOT use expect's
# `log_file FILENAME` -- it aborts on macOS expect 5.45 with "Tcl_RegisterChannel:
# duplicate channel names" (the opened log channel collides with the spawn pty).

# Pure-Tcl repo-root derivation -- NO `exec` (an exec before spawn is one of
# several macOS expect 5.45 footguns; this file lives at tools/interactive/).
# --- #60: make the byte stream byte-transparent -----------------------------
#
# THE CUT, ROOT-CAUSED (2026-07-28). expect was decoding the guest's serial
# stream as UTF-8, and when a read landed mid-way through a multi-byte
# character it reported EOF instead of waiting for the rest -- so the scenario
# died with "unexpected EOF" while the VM was alive and the relay was healthy.
#
# The trace caught it exactly (`exp_internal`, post-login so the boot flood
# stayed out of it):
#     send: sending "ls /\r" to { exp6 }
#     send: sending "echo T60MARK40\r" to { exp6 }
#     expect: does "...[38;2;224;120;64m " match "T60MARK40"? no
#     expect: read eof
# Both sends succeeded; the last read stopped precisely at the byte before
# `⊢` (U+22A2, 3 bytes) in ut's prompt, and the next event was EOF. That the
# trace prints `⊢` rather than raw bytes is itself the proof that the
# channel was being DECODED: `fconfigure -translation binary` is applied to
# the pipe below, but `spawn -open` does not preserve it.
#
# This explains every property of the bug that made it look like magic: it
# lands "always on a character boundary"; it correlates with bursts of 3-byte
# glyphs (ut redraws the ⊢ prompt on EVERY keystroke, so a send is followed by
# a burst of them -- hence "cuts land at or just after a send"); it is
# probabilistic (it needs a read to split a character); and it is untouched by
# -buffersize (probe 2) and by match_max, neither of which is in that path.
#
# iso8859-1 makes every byte a valid single-byte character, so a partial
# sequence cannot exist and the decoder can never be caught mid-character.
# Safe here: every pattern the scenarios match is ASCII (checked across all
# 32 -- the only non-ASCII in this tree is in comments like this one).
#
# A/B, 120 rounds of bursty output each: default encoding cut at round 40 and
# round 86; iso8859-1 completed 120/120 twice. Set BEFORE any channel is
# opened, which is why it lives at the top of this file.
encoding system iso8859-1

set ::lc_repo [file normalize [file join [file dirname [info script]] .. ..]]
set ::lc_boot_timeout [expr {[info exists ::env(LS_CI_BOOT_TIMEOUT)] ? $::env(LS_CI_BOOT_TIMEOUT) : 180}]
set ::lc_cmd_timeout  [expr {[info exists ::env(LS_CI_CMD_TIMEOUT)]  ? $::env(LS_CI_CMD_TIMEOUT)  : 30}]

# Flush-immune progress marker: open/puts/close flushes on close, so the file
# is current even though expect's own stdout is block-buffered.
proc lc_step {msg} {
    if {[info exists ::env(LS_CI_STEPS)]} {
        catch {
            set fh [open $::env(LS_CI_STEPS) a]
            puts $fh $msg
            close $fh
        }
    }
}

# --- #78 expect-side instrumentation -------------------------------------
#
# The bridge instrument (b0ffd862) exonerated the relay: at every cut it was
# perfectly current (~124 KB delivered, 1-5 bytes outstanding, ZERO idle in
# BOTH directions) and reported `stdin_eof=1` -- the BENIGN teardown shape.
# So expect closes its own spawn channel, mid-scenario, while the guest is
# alive (stat R+) and the relay is healthy. All observed cuts land at or just
# after a `send`, i.e. when expect WRITES.
#
# expect cannot report that to us by default: a failed `send` is a Tcl error
# that unwinds past the scenario, and the resulting symptom downstream is a
# bare EOF or timeout with the cause discarded. These two hooks keep it.

# `exp_internal -f FILE 0` writes expect's OWN diagnostics (every byte sent,
# every byte read, every match attempt, and the channel/EOF events) to FILE
# and NOT to stderr -- so the transcript stays readable.
#
# OPT-IN, AND DELIBERATELY SO -- the trace is O(reads x buffer), NOT O(bytes):
# expect re-prints the ENTIRE accumulated match buffer on EVERY match attempt,
# and this harness runs `match_max 200000`. A full boot is hundreds of reads
# against a buffer that can reach 200 KB, so an unbounded trace is a
# multi-GB-per-attempt hazard, not a "~1 MB" one. A runaway log has already
# ENOSPC-bricked this host once. Enable it for a single targeted repro, watch
# the file, and never leave it on for a full-set or gate run.
proc lc_trace_init {} {
    if {![info exists ::env(LS_CI_EXPECT_TRACE)]} return
    if {$::env(LS_CI_EXPECT_TRACE) eq "0"} return
    set ::lc_trace /tmp/thyla-exptrace-[pid].log
    file delete -force $::lc_trace
    if {[catch {exp_internal -f $::lc_trace 0} err]} {
        lc_step "TRACE: could not enable exp_internal ($err)"
        unset ::lc_trace
        return
    }
    lc_step "TRACE: exp_internal -> $::lc_trace"
}

# Move the trace next to the attempt evidence so a retry cannot destroy it
# (the #72 lesson: the no-host-load discipline needs the artifact to LOOK at).
proc lc_trace_preserve {} {
    if {![info exists ::lc_trace] || ![file exists $::lc_trace]} return
    # The pid keeps each ATTEMPT's trace distinct -- a fixed name would let a
    # retry destroy the first failing attempt's evidence, which is the exact
    # #72 mistake (a retry is a tolerance, never a diagnosis, and the evidence
    # has to survive to be read).
    set dst $::lc_repo/build/ls-ci-[file rootname [file tail $::argv0]].exptrace.[pid].log
    if {[catch {file copy -force $::lc_trace $dst} err]} {
        lc_step "TRACE: preserve failed ($err); live at $::lc_trace"
        return
    }
    lc_step "TRACE: preserved -> $dst ([file size $dst] bytes)"
}

# Every write to the guest goes through here. A `send` that fails is the
# single most informative event in the #78 hunt, and it is exactly the one
# expect throws away by default.
# --- infrastructure-vs-guest classification (the #74 fail-open lesson) ------
#
# MEASURED 2026-07-27: a full-set run reported 30/32 scenarios as "deterministic
# = a real regression, not a flake". Ground truth from the vm logs was that 84
# of 177 boots never started at all -- QEMU refused with
#   -device virtio-blk-device,drive=pool0: Failed to get "write" lock
# because another QEMU still held build/fixtures/pool.img. EVERY scenario shares
# that one fixture, and 12 gfx scenarios legitimately switch to accel=hvf, so a
# single lingering VM cascades into a whole run of bogus failures that look
# exactly like a guest regression.
#
# The harness must never present "I could not run" as "the guest is broken".
# This records the VM's OWN words in the steps under an INFRA: marker; the
# wrapper keys on it to classify the attempt.
proc lc_vm_infra_check {} {
    if {![info exists ::lc_vmlog] || ![file exists $::lc_vmlog]} return
    if {[catch {
        set f [open $::lc_vmlog r]
        set d [read $f]
        close $f
    }]} return
    foreach {pat what} {
        {Failed to get "write" lock}  "another QEMU holds a drive image (stale VM? concurrent run?)"
        {Failed to bind socket}       "a stale socket file blocks the QMP/serial bind"
        {Address already in use}      "a port/socket is already taken by another VM"
        {could not open disk image}   "a drive image is missing or unreadable"
    } {
        if {[string first $pat $d] >= 0} {
            lc_step "INFRA: $what -- the VM never started, so this attempt says NOTHING about the guest"
            foreach line [split $d "\n"] {
                if {[string first "qemu-system" $line] >= 0 &&
                    ([string first "Failed" $line] >= 0 ||
                     [string first "could not" $line] >= 0)} {
                    lc_step "INFRA: qemu: [string trim $line]"
                }
            }
            return
        }
    }
}

# --- #127: collision-free VNC display allocation --------------------------
#
# The counterpart to the "Address already in use" arm above: that one NAMES
# the collision after the VM is already dead, this one keeps it from
# happening.
#
# A scenario needing a REAL display backend (ls-gfx-live) boots QEMU with
# `-display vnc=127.0.0.1:N` and then attaches a client to 127.0.0.1:5900+N,
# so both ends need the same N AND the harness has to know it -- which is why
# QEMU's own `to=` range-scan is not usable here: it picks a port and never
# tells us which.
#
# N was the constant 47. This host runs two trees at once (main + the aux
# worktree, CLAUDE.md "Parallel auxiliary track"); both computed 47, both
# wanted TCP 5947, and whichever booted second died with "Failed to find an
# available port". Retrying bought NOTHING -- a deterministic collision
# re-picks the same port every attempt, so the budget just spent three boots
# before reporting (observed 3/3, #127).
#
# Two defences, closing DIFFERENT holes -- neither alone is enough:
#   base  -- derived from THIS tree's absolute path (the #89 move), so two
#            trees begin at different displays and never contend at all.
#   probe -- bind-test upward from base, so a stale QEMU still holding our
#            base is stepped over instead of collided with.
# The probe has a real TOCTOU window (we close the socket; QEMU binds it a
# second later), which is exactly why the cross-tree case is carried by the
# base separation and not by the probe.
#
# Range 32..95 -> TCP 5932..5995: below 32 is where humans and macOS Screen
# Sharing live (5900), and 6000+ is X11.
# The QMP control socket 14 gfx scenarios drive. It was `$repo/build/qmp.sock`
# hardcoded in all 14, which is one fixed host resource shared by every
# scenario -- a deterministic collision the moment two run at once (#127's
# lesson, applied before it can bite rather than after). The wrapper points
# THYLACINE_QMP_SOCK at the scenario's own slot; run-vm.sh reads the same
# variable, so both ends agree by construction. Unset, this is exactly the old
# path, so a hand-run scenario behaves as it always did.
proc lc_qmp_sock {} {
    if {[info exists ::env(THYLACINE_QMP_SOCK)] && $::env(THYLACINE_QMP_SOCK) ne ""} {
        return $::env(THYLACINE_QMP_SOCK)
    }
    return "$::lc_repo/build/qmp.sock"
}

proc lc_vnc_probe_noop {args} {}

proc lc_port_bindable {port} {
    if {[catch {set s [socket -server lc_vnc_probe_noop -myaddr 127.0.0.1 $port]}]} {
        return 0
    }
    close $s
    return 1
}

proc lc_pick_vnc_display {} {
    if {[info exists ::env(LS_CI_VNC_DISPLAY)]} {
        # Manual override: honoured verbatim, NOT probed -- if you pinned it,
        # you want that exact display and a silent slide elsewhere would be
        # the opposite of helpful.
        return $::env(LS_CI_VNC_DISPLAY)
    }
    set lo 32
    set span 64
    # djb2 over the repo path (Tcl exposes no string hash), so the starting
    # point is stable for a tree and different between trees.
    set h 5381
    foreach ch [split $::lc_repo ""] {
        set h [expr {(($h * 33) + [scan $ch %c]) % 1000003}]
    }
    set base [expr {$h % $span}]
    # G-2: a third separation, for scenarios of the SAME tree running at once.
    # The tree hash separates trees and the probe steps over stragglers, but
    # concurrent scenarios here all hash to one base and would then race each
    # other through the probe's TOCTOU window -- the one gap the comment above
    # says the base, not the probe, is supposed to cover. The wrapper hands each
    # slot its index, so concurrent scenarios start at different displays and
    # the probe goes back to being the backstop it was designed as.
    if {[info exists ::env(LS_CI_VNC_BASE)]
        && [string is integer -strict $::env(LS_CI_VNC_BASE)]} {
        set base [expr {($base + $::env(LS_CI_VNC_BASE)) % $span}]
    }
    for {set i 0} {$i < $span} {incr i} {
        set d [expr {$lo + (($base + $i) % $span)}]
        if {[lc_port_bindable [expr {5900 + $d}]]} {
            lc_step "VNC display :$d (TCP [expr {5900 + $d}]); tree base :[expr {$lo + $base}]"
            return $d
        }
    }
    lc_fail "no free VNC display in :$lo..:[expr {$lo + $span - 1}] (TCP 5900+N) -- $span consecutive ports taken"
}

proc lc_raw_send {bytes what} {
    if {[catch {send -- $bytes} err]} {
        lc_step "SEND-ERROR during \[$what\]: $err"
        lc_step "SEND-ERROR errorCode: $::errorCode"
        lc_step "SEND-ERROR errorInfo: [string map {\n { | }} $::errorInfo]"
        lc_fail "send failed during '$what': $err"
    }
}

# Fail with the guest's OWN reason attached.
#
# An arm that matches a bare failure PREFIX (the battery's "tapestry-battery:
# FAIL") consumes only the prefix; the reason is still unread in the buffer
# when lc_fail exits, so it is lost. Ground truth (#82, ls-gfx-panes): three
# preserved attempts ended at exactly "FAIL " / "FAIL s" -- the reason
# ("synth rel never arrived" vs "synth rel got (dx,dy)", two very different
# bugs) was raced away by our own teardown, leaving every gfx failure
# reasonless. A check that cannot say WHY is barely a check (#74).
#
# The pattern REQUIRES the line terminator: matching any run of bytes would
# return whatever happened to have arrived (" s") and reproduce the truncation
# it exists to fix.
# NOTE the timeout is set via the GLOBAL, not `expect -timeout 5 {...}`: expect
# 5.45 (macOS) does not accept -timeout as an option here and silently matches
# NOTHING, so the capture always fell through to "no reason" -- the exact
# fail-open this proc exists to close. Caught by exercising the failure path
# directly before committing; the passing path never runs it.
proc lc_fail_detail {msg} {
    global timeout
    set lc_saved_timeout $timeout
    set timeout 5
    set detail ""
    expect {
        -re {^([^\r\n]*)[\r\n]} { set detail [string trim $expect_out(1,string)] }
        timeout {}
        eof {}
    }
    set timeout $lc_saved_timeout
    if {$detail ne ""} {
        lc_fail "$msg -- guest reason: $detail"
    } else {
        lc_fail "$msg (guest gave no reason within 5s)"
    }
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The failure-time state probe -- a scenario's OWN account of the guest at the
# moment its assertion missed.
#
# WHY: pty-4 burned a retry (2026-08-16) at "^Z stops the foreground sleep":
# the echo stopped at `sleep ` and `Stopped` never came, guest ALIVE. Two
# causes fit that capture EXACTLY -- the guest received `sleep ` + Enter (INPUT
# truncation: sleep exited at once, no job to stop) or it received `sleep 30`
# and stopped correctly while the pts output was lost (OUTPUT loss) -- and a
# third fits too (the ^Z byte itself lost: sleep 30 RUNNING in the foreground,
# nobody reading the terminal). Raising sensitivity on the capture cannot
# separate them; only a SECOND AXIS can. The guest can be ASKED: `jobs` says
# whether a stopped job exists; a ^C then `jobs` says whether a foreground job
# was running; an uppercase-pipe token says whether the channel is alive at all.
#
# So a scenario sets `::lc_fail_probe` to a script, and lc_fail runs it ONCE,
# before the kill, only while the VM is alive. It RECORDS, it does not assert:
# every byte the guest answers is logged (control bytes escaped) as
# `fail-probe:` steps, and the scenario's comment says how to read them. A
# probe that fails must not recurse into lc_fail -- so it is cleared before it
# runs, and lc_probe_capture never calls lc_fail (it uses a bare expect).
#
# NOT a retry and NOT a tolerance: it changes nothing on the passing path (a
# probe set but never needed costs nothing) and turns "the next occurrence
# will have to be re-derived" into "the next occurrence is decidable from the
# log". Same principle as the compaction ledger: make the miss AUDIBLE.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
set ::lc_fail_probe {}

# Send `bytes` (may be empty: just listen), then record everything the guest
# answers within `secs` seconds of quiet. Bounded twice: by the quiet timeout
# and by a chunk cap, so a streaming guest cannot hold the probe open.
proc lc_probe_capture {bytes secs what} {
    global timeout
    set saved $timeout
    set timeout $secs
    if {$bytes ne ""} {
        if {[catch {send -- $bytes} err]} {
            lc_step "fail-probe: send failed for \[$what\]: $err"
            set timeout $saved
            return
        }
    }
    set buf ""
    set chunks 0
    expect {
        -re {.+} {
            append buf $expect_out(0,string)
            incr chunks
            if {$chunks < 200} { exp_continue }
        }
        timeout {}
        eof { append buf "<EOF>" }
    }
    set timeout $saved
    # Escape so the log is one greppable line per probe: ESC and other control
    # bytes become <xx>; ANSI sequences stay visible instead of repainting the
    # log.
    set shown ""
    foreach ch [split $buf ""] {
        scan $ch %c code
        if {$code < 32 || $code == 127} {
            append shown [format "<%02x>" $code]
        } else {
            append shown $ch
        }
    }
    lc_step "fail-probe: \[$what\] -> ${chunks} chunk(s): $shown"
}

proc lc_fail_probe_run {} {
    if {![info exists ::lc_fail_probe] || $::lc_fail_probe eq ""} return
    set probe $::lc_fail_probe
    set ::lc_fail_probe {}          ;# one-shot: no recursion through lc_fail
    if {![info exists ::lc_vm_pid]} return
    if {[catch {exec ps -p $::lc_vm_pid -o stat=} st]} return
    if {[string match {Z*} [string trim $st]]} return
    lc_step "fail-probe: begin (VM alive; asking the guest for its own account)"
    if {[catch {uplevel #0 $probe} err]} {
        lc_step "fail-probe: script errored: $err"
    }
    lc_step "fail-probe: end"
}

proc lc_fail {msg} {
    lc_step "FAIL: $msg"
    lc_fail_probe_run
    # Evidence BEFORE the kill: the VM's process state at failure time splits
    # "qemu exited" (stat Z* -- Tcl lazy-reaps, so a dead child lingers as a
    # zombie; kill -0 would lie) from "the serial bridge died under a LIVE VM"
    # (stat R/S/U). Without this the two are indistinguishable at the expect
    # layer -- both surface as EOF -- which is how the bridge-death class hid
    # behind a "qemu exited before login prompt" message for so long.
    if {[info exists ::lc_vm_pid]} {
        if {[catch {exec ps -p $::lc_vm_pid -o stat=} st]} { set st GONE }
        lc_step "vm-at-fail: pid $::lc_vm_pid stat=[string trim $st]"
    }
    # A dead bridge leaves its exit record here; a live one leaves the file
    # empty. rc 141 == 128+SIGPIPE.
    #
    # #125: this read happens while the relay is still ALIVE (lc_vm_kill is
    # below), so the EXIT record is normally absent -- but a relay STALL is
    # written here immediately and flushed the moment it is seen, precisely so
    # it survives to this point. That makes the empty case load-bearing
    # evidence, not an absence: it says the console consumer was running, so a
    # guest that stopped making progress was NOT frozen from the host side
    # (QEMU's serial write blocks after only ~8 KiB of undrained output and
    # suspends the whole VM -- tools/stall-amplify.sh). Say that OUT LOUD
    # rather than leaving it to be inferred from a missing line, which is
    # indistinguishable from the instrument not having run at all.
    if {[info exists ::lc_ncerr] && [file exists $::lc_ncerr]} {
        catch {
            set f [open $::lc_ncerr r]
            set d [string trim [read $f]]
            close $f
            if {$d ne ""} {
                lc_step "bridge-at-fail: $d"
            } else {
                lc_step "bridge-at-fail: (record empty -- relay ALIVE and reported NO stall; the console channel did not freeze the VM)"
            }
        }
    } else {
        lc_step "bridge-at-fail: (no relay record -- transport=pty, or the bridge never started)"
    }
    # A VM that never started is NOT a scenario failure. Say so, loudly, in the
    # steps -- the wrapper keys on the INFRA: marker to stop calling it "a real
    # regression". See lc_vm_infra_check.
    lc_vm_infra_check
    lc_trace_preserve
    send_user "\nLS-CI FAIL: $msg\n"
    lc_vm_kill
    exit 1
}

proc lc_pass {msg} {
    lc_step "PASS: $msg"
    send_user "\nLS-CI PASS: $msg\n"
}

# Spawn the VM via the canonical launcher. log_user stays on (default) so the
# wrapper's stdout redirect captures the full boot+session as the transcript;
# the steps file is the flush-immune live view.
proc lc_boot {} {
    # `global spawn_id` is load-bearing: `spawn` writes spawn_id in the CURRENT
    # scope, so without this the spawn would be local to lc_boot and every later
    # proc's expect/send would find no open spawn (instant spurious EOF).
    global lc_repo lc_boot_timeout timeout spawn_id
    set accel [expr {[info exists ::env(THYLACINE_ACCEL)] ? $::env(THYLACINE_ACCEL) : "tcg"}]
    set timeout $lc_boot_timeout
    # #66: on macOS (26.4) a reader parked in poll()/select() on an
    # expect-spawn pty can LOSE the master-write wakeup: the bytes queue in the
    # slave (FIONREAD grows per keystroke) while the parked reader never wakes
    # -- qemu's stdin deafens a few seconds after its main loop goes
    # timer-quiet, and every TUI scenario wedges (the ls-7 regression; full
    # hunt: memory/bug_66_console_poll_wake_lost.md). So on Darwin the serial
    # console (incl. the qemu monitor mux) rides a UNIX SOCKET, bridged into
    # the expect pty by serial-bridge.py -- a bounded-park pump whose periodic
    # re-entry level-checks the pty, immune to lost wakeups by construction.
    # LS_CI_TRANSPORT=pty forces the direct spawn (Linux CI / A-B debugging);
    # LS_CI_TRANSPORT=sock forces the bridge anywhere.
    set transport [expr {[info exists ::env(LS_CI_TRANSPORT)] \
        ? $::env(LS_CI_TRANSPORT) \
        : ([string equal $::tcl_platform(os) "Darwin"] ? "sock" : "pty")}]
    if {$transport eq "sock"} {
        set sock /tmp/thyla-serial-[pid].sock
        # Global: lc_vm_infra_check reads it on failure to tell a VM that never
        # started from a guest that misbehaved.
        set ::lc_vmlog /tmp/thyla-vm-[pid].log
        set vmlog $::lc_vmlog
        file delete -force $sock
        lc_step "BOOT vm accel=$accel transport=sock timeout=${lc_boot_timeout}s"
        # server (wait) mode: qemu blocks until the bridge connects, so no
        # early boot output is lost. The bg pid is run-vm.sh's, which execs
        # qemu in place -- lc_fail/lc_quit kill exactly this VM, never a
        # parallel job's.
        #
        # #125: WE create the listening socket, not qemu, and hand it the fd.
        # A host-side stall of the relay blocks qemu's serial write and
        # SUSPENDS the guest, and the capacity that buys is the WRITER's
        # SO_SNDBUF -- qemu's socket, which only its listener's setting can
        # size. serial-listen.py sets it before listen() and execs through, so
        # the accepted connection inherits 8 MiB instead of 8192 B. MEASURED,
        # nobody reading for 60 s: 44221 B and no login (qemu's listener, and
        # byte-identical to the 12 s figure -- a hard stop) vs 128183 B and a
        # login prompt (ours). Full rationale + the vacuous-fix trap that
        # precedes it: serial-listen.py's header.
        #
        # `env` and run-vm.sh both exec in place, so ::lc_vm_pid still names
        # exactly one process all the way down to qemu. {SERIALFD} is literal
        # here -- Tcl only treats braces as quoting at the START of a word.
        set ::lc_vm_pid [exec python3 $lc_repo/tools/interactive/serial-listen.py \
            --sock $sock -- \
            env THYLACINE_ACCEL=$accel THYLACINE_SERIAL=chardev:ser0 \
            $lc_repo/tools/run-vm.sh \
            -chardev socket,id=ser0,fd={SERIALFD},server=on,wait=on,mux=on \
            -mon chardev=ser0,mode=readline \
            > $vmlog 2>@1 &]
        # This now proves serial-listen.py BOUND the socket -- it no longer
        # proves qemu came up, because the listener exists before qemu is
        # exec'd. A qemu that dies at startup is caught instead at the relay's
        # connect(): its fds close with it, the listener is destroyed, and the
        # connect is REFUSED -- which the bridge reports as
        # `reason=connect-refused` and lc_fail surfaces as bridge-at-fail.
        # That fires when the bridge actually connects, so unlike a liveness
        # poll here it cannot race a slow-starting qemu.
        for {set i 0} {$i < 150} {incr i} {
            if {[file exists $sock]} break
            after 200
        }
        if {![file exists $sock]} { lc_fail "serial socket never appeared (vm log: $vmlog)" }
        # PIPE bridge, not a pty: `spawn -open` drives an already-open Tcl
        # pipe channel to the relay, so NO pty exists anywhere on the input
        # path (expect -> pipe -> bridge -> unix socket -> qemu). Every failing
        # configuration in the #66 hunt shared an expect-spawn pty as the
        # input carrier; pipes have none of the tty layer.
        #
        # #72 FIX: the relay is serial-bridge.py, NOT `nc -U`. MEASURED on this
        # harness: `nc -U` lost 5 of 10 single-attempt boots, every one of them
        # with the VM still ALIVE (stat R+/S+) and `bridge exit=141` -- BSD nc
        # taking SIGPIPE mid-boot. expect saw the resulting EOF and lib.exp's
        # login `eof` arm ASSERTED "qemu exited before login prompt", which is
        # how a harness bug spent this long being logged as a guest/host-timing
        # flake. serial-bridge.py is immune by construction: SIGPIPE is
        # Python-default-ignored (a transient stdout condition raises a
        # CATCHABLE BrokenPipeError), stdout writes block for back-pressure
        # instead of racing, a bounded select() park re-checks both endpoints
        # level-triggered (also covering the #66 lost-wakeup class), and stdin
        # EOF does NOT end the relay (BSD nc exits there).
        #
        # #72 instrument: the bridge reports `bridge exit=<rc> reason=<why>` on
        # stderr; lc_fail surfaces it as bridge-at-fail, alongside vm-at-fail.
        # Together they split "the bridge died" from "the guest died" -- expect
        # sees the same bare EOF either way, so without these two the cause is
        # unknowable and the temptation is to guess.
        #
        # HOW TO READ `reason=` (#78 -- the record is now discriminating; the
        # relay header has the full rationale):
        #   stdout-broken       expect had ALREADY closed stdin -> it was tearing
        #                       down. This is the NORMAL end-of-session reason
        #                       (a passing scenario ends exactly this way), so on
        #                       a failure it is nearly contentless -- do NOT read
        #                       it as a cause. It was read as one for three
        #                       sessions of #78.
        #   stdout-broken-live  expect was ALIVE (stdin still open) yet its read
        #                       end was gone. THIS is the anomaly worth chasing.
        #   socket-eof          QEMU closed the serial -- the guest really ended.
        #   spool-overflow      a genuinely wedged reader (64 MiB undelivered).
        #   connect-refused     the listener was already gone when the relay
        #                       connected -- i.e. qemu died at startup. Since
        #                       #125 WE create the listener, so the socket FILE
        #                       existing no longer implies a live qemu; this is
        #                       the signal that replaced it. Read the vm log.
        #
        # READ `stalls=` FIRST ON ANY SILENT-GUEST FAILURE (#125). It counts
        # wake-to-wake gaps > 1 s in the relay's own loop -- i.e. windows where
        # the relay was OFF-CPU and therefore not draining QEMU's serial socket.
        # That socket holds only 8192 bytes (macOS; its capacity is QEMU's
        # SO_SNDBUF, which nothing on our side can raise), versus ~117-198 KiB
        # of output per boot -- so a stall past ~8 KiB of guest output BLOCKS
        # QEMU's write and SUSPENDS the guest. MEASURED: SIGSTOP the relay and
        # qemu's host CPU goes 100% -> 2.4% in ~2 s (tools/stall-amplify.sh).
        #   stalls=0   the relay was running -- a silent guest is NOT explained
        #              by the console channel; look at the guest.
        #   stalls>0   the VM was frozen from the HOST side for max_stall
        #              seconds. That is infrastructure, not a guest defect.
        # Individual stalls are also written to this same stderr log as they
        # happen (`bridge STALL gap=...`), so the evidence survives the relay
        # being killed on a scenario timeout -- which is when it matters most.
        # The counters (in/out/spool/idle_in/idle_out/up) size the burst the cut
        # landed in, so the buffer-pressure correlation is checkable from the
        # record rather than re-derived by hand each time.
        set ::lc_ncerr /tmp/thyla-ncerr-[pid].log
        file delete -force $::lc_ncerr
        set bridge $lc_repo/tools/interactive/serial-bridge.py
        set chan [open [list | python3 $bridge $sock 2> $::lc_ncerr] r+]
        # #60 probe 2 verdict (2026-07-23): -buffersize 262144 was trialed
        # against ls-gfx-mode's then-deterministic 47-char-echo cut -- it
        # MOVED the cut off that leg (buffer-pressure correlation confirmed)
        # but scattered later cuts across shorter commands with no net
        # attempt-rate win, and >match_max is its own hazard. Reverted to
        # the default; the durable mitigations are scenario-side (short
        # typed commands -- the echo burst is quadratic in line length) +
        # the attempts retry. Full evidence: memory bug-60 file.
        fconfigure $chan -buffering none -translation binary
        spawn -open $chan
        # #72 residual: expect's default match_max is 2000 bytes, so a ~110 KB
        # boot burst forces ~55 discard-and-rescan cycles. Every observed
        # `stdout-broken` cut landed inside the heaviest of those bursts (a run
        # of 3-byte U+2500 box-drawing), always on a character boundary. Give
        # the matcher room rather than churning it.
        match_max 200000
        lc_step "BOOT serial-bridge.py up"
    } else {
        lc_step "BOOT spawn accel=$accel timeout=${lc_boot_timeout}s"
        spawn env THYLACINE_ACCEL=$accel $lc_repo/tools/run-vm.sh
    }
    lc_trace_init
}

# Kill the socket-transport VM if one was started (no-op otherwise). The pty
# transport's VM dies with its spawn; the socket VM is a background process
# that must be reaped explicitly on both the pass and fail exits.
proc lc_vm_kill {} {
    if {[info exists ::lc_vm_pid]} {
        catch {exec kill -9 $::lc_vm_pid}
        unset ::lc_vm_pid
    }
}

# An EXTINCTION's Halls dump (symbolized backtrace) FOLLOWS the marker on the
# serial; bailing at the token loses it (task #58: `EXTINCTION: pw_wake:` with
# the message + dump truncated -- the one artifact a nondeterministic repro
# needs). Drain a few seconds of serial first so the dump lands in the attempt
# log, then fail.
proc lc_extinction_fail {msg} {
    catch { expect -timeout 5 timeout {} eof {} }
    lc_fail $msg
}

# Wait for an EXACT string, with extinction / timeout / eof as hard failures.
# `phase` is a label for the failure message. Uses the command timeout.
proc lc_expect {pat phase} {
    global lc_cmd_timeout timeout
    set timeout $lc_cmd_timeout
    expect {
        -ex $pat        { lc_step "saw: $phase" }
        -re {EXTINCTION:} { lc_extinction_fail "kernel extinction during '$phase'" }
        timeout         { lc_fail "timeout (${lc_cmd_timeout}s) waiting for \[$pat\] during '$phase'" }
        eof             { lc_fail "unexpected EOF during '$phase'" }
    }
}

# The REGEX twin of lc_expect: for anchors whose PROOF is in what precedes the
# text (e.g. "plain text directly after a CRLF" -- a cooked pts echo -- versus the
# same text inside an editor redraw, behind a prompt). Same failure discipline.
proc lc_expect_re {pat phase} {
    global lc_cmd_timeout timeout
    set timeout $lc_cmd_timeout
    expect {
        -re $pat        { lc_step "saw: $phase" }
        -re {EXTINCTION:} { lc_extinction_fail "kernel extinction during '$phase'" }
        timeout         { lc_fail "timeout (${lc_cmd_timeout}s) waiting for regex \[$pat\] during '$phase'" }
        eof             { lc_fail "unexpected EOF during '$phase'" }
    }
}

# Type a command line + Enter (CR -- a raw terminal sends CR; LS-1 made login +
# the editor accept it).
proc lc_send {line} {
    lc_step "send: $line"
    lc_raw_send "$line\r" "send: $line"
}

# Send a raw Ctrl-C (byte 0x03). The kernel console RX cooks it to the
# `interrupt` note (kernel/cons.c); Ctrl-A (0x01) stays the qemu monitor escape,
# so 0x03 passes through to the guest. Used by the LS-5 scenarios.
proc lc_ctrlc {} {
    lc_step "send Ctrl-C (0x03)"
    lc_raw_send "\x03" "Ctrl-C"
}

# Run `cmd` and assert its OUTPUT contains `expected` (exact). The editor does
# not emit typed input as plain text (see "Echo discipline" above), so we match
# the OUTPUT directly -- no echo-consume step. Choose `expected` so it cannot
# appear in the typed line (e.g. a value the command computes, not echoes).
proc lc_run_expect {cmd expected} {
    lc_send $cmd
    lc_expect $expected "output-of: $cmd"
}

# Like lc_run_expect, but a line matching `failpat` fails the scenario
# IMMEDIATELY instead of waiting out the timeout. For provers that print
# their own FAIL line and return to the prompt: without a fail arm their
# red path reports only via the timeout arm (#218 close F2 -- the warp
# battery's red latency was the full lc_cmd_timeout). Keying a RED verdict
# on the failure string is the safe direction of #186: a spurious match
# turns a pass into a fail, never the reverse.
proc lc_run_expect_hardfail {cmd expected failpat} {
    global lc_cmd_timeout timeout
    lc_send $cmd
    set timeout $lc_cmd_timeout
    expect {
        -ex $expected     { lc_step "saw: output-of: $cmd" }
        -ex $failpat      { lc_fail "hard-fail line \[$failpat\] during 'output-of: $cmd'" }
        -re {EXTINCTION:} { lc_extinction_fail "kernel extinction during 'output-of: $cmd'" }
        timeout           { lc_fail "timeout (${lc_cmd_timeout}s) waiting for \[$expected\] during 'output-of: $cmd'" }
        eof               { lc_fail "unexpected EOF during 'output-of: $cmd'" }
    }
}

# Like lc_run_expect_hardfail, but `failre` is a REGEXP, so several red lines
# can share the one arm (a prover's own `FAIL --` token AND its scenario-level
# `INCOMPLETE(<arm>)` verdict, C-0d Fable round F6). Same direction as the -ex
# sibling: a spurious match turns a pass into a fail, never the reverse. The
# matched text is reported, since the pattern alone no longer says which line.
proc lc_run_expect_hardfail_re {cmd expected failre} {
    global lc_cmd_timeout timeout
    lc_send $cmd
    set timeout $lc_cmd_timeout
    expect {
        -ex $expected     { lc_step "saw: output-of: $cmd" }
        -re $failre       { lc_fail "hard-fail line \[$expect_out(0,string)\] during 'output-of: $cmd'" }
        -re {EXTINCTION:} { lc_extinction_fail "kernel extinction during 'output-of: $cmd'" }
        timeout           { lc_fail "timeout (${lc_cmd_timeout}s) waiting for \[$expected\] during 'output-of: $cmd'" }
        eof               { lc_fail "unexpected EOF during 'output-of: $cmd'" }
    }
}

# Log in over the console. Reaching the shell banner proves the kernel received
# every keystroke (the LS-1 regression) and that login authenticated.
proc lc_login {user pass} {
    global lc_boot_timeout timeout
    set timeout $lc_boot_timeout
    expect {
        -ex "Thylacine login: " { lc_step "login prompt" }
        -re {EXTINCTION:}       { lc_extinction_fail "extinction before login prompt" }
        timeout                 { lc_fail "no login prompt within ${lc_boot_timeout}s" }
        eof                     { lc_fail "qemu exited before login prompt" }
    }
    lc_step "send user: $user"
    lc_raw_send "$user\r" "login user"
    expect {
        -ex "password: "  { lc_step "password prompt" }
        -re {EXTINCTION:} { lc_extinction_fail "extinction at password prompt" }
        timeout           { lc_fail "no password prompt (username not received?)" }
        eof               { lc_fail "qemu exited at password prompt" }
    }
    lc_step "send password (masked)"
    lc_raw_send "$pass\r" "login password"
    expect {
        -ex "Thylacine textual shell" { lc_step "shell up -- login OK (LS-1)" }
        -ex "login incorrect"         { lc_fail "auth rejected for $user -- the credential is known-good, so suspect #233 FIRST: a byte that reaches the console between login's password prompt and its noecho consctl flip is echoed AND then discarded by the flip's TCSAFLUSH line-reset, truncating the passphrase. Look for an echoed character immediately after 'password: ' in the preserved log; that is the fingerprint. Fixed by ordering (mode set BEFORE the prompt) -- a recurrence means the window reopened." }
        -re {EXTINCTION:}             { lc_extinction_fail "extinction during login/provision" }
        timeout                       { lc_fail "shell never came up after auth (login/provision stall?)" }
        eof                           { lc_fail "qemu exited during login" }
    }
    # Let the first prompt settle before driving commands.
    sleep 1
}

# Clean shutdown: leave the shell, then quit qemu via the monitor escape
# (Ctrl-A x). The wrapper reaps any lingering qemu regardless.
proc lc_quit {} {
    global lc_cmd_timeout timeout
    set timeout $lc_cmd_timeout
    lc_send "exit"
    sleep 1
    lc_step "quit qemu (Ctrl-A x)"
    # NOT lc_raw_send: at teardown the channel is expected to be going away,
    # so a write error here is benign and must not turn a PASS into a FAIL.
    catch {send -- "\001x"}
    expect {
        eof     { lc_step "EOF clean" }
        timeout { lc_step "EOF timeout (wrapper will reap qemu)" }
    }
    lc_vm_kill
}
